abstract: >-
  SiO2 is one of the most extensively studied materials at high pressure, and
  yet experimental investigations of the melt curve have yielded contradictory
  results. Using a two-stage light gas gun, we measured shock temperatures in
  the visible to mid-infrared on α-quartz and fused silica across the proposed
  range for the melt curve. In this region, the shock Hugoniots of both
  materials exhibit a well-known temperature inversion, a sawtooth feature in
  temperature-pressure space, that has been interpreted as superheating above
  the melt curve. We collected thermal emission at visible to mid-infrared
  wavelengths and find a change in the optical attenuation length at the
  temperature inversion. Based on a compendium of data including decaying
  shock experiments at OMEGA, we explain the anomalous inversion by the
  formation of a cool metastable melt layer in the shock front above 114 and
  74 GPa on the α-quartz and fused silica Hugoniots, respectively. We
  constructed a model melt curve and find that the temperature inversion
  occurs near the solidus density, and the α-quartz and fused silica
  Hugoniots enter the liquid field at about 146 and 91 GPa, respectively. We
  demonstrate that the shock front is semi-transparent below the inversion.
  SiO2 is an end-member composition for planetary materials, and its equation
  of state is important for planet formation, evolution, and impact
  processes. Our reinterpretation of the shock temperatures supports a higher
  temperature melt curve than proposed in previous shock studies.
keywords:
  - SiO2
  - quartz
  - fused silica
  - shock temperature
  - melt curve
  - equation of state
